markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Facilitating Testing Classifiers

## What's Actually Happening in This Lesson

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">1</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Comparing Classifiers:</strong> students calculate accuracy, recall, and precision for two berry-toxicity classifiers, Model A and Model B, from the Activity Guide</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">2</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Tuning Classifiers (Levels 1-2):</strong> students tune a confidence threshold for a context of their own choosing, then justify the threshold they picked</div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

## Watch for: "Higher Accuracy Means Better Model"

<div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; margin: 20px 0; flex-wrap: wrap;">
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #FFE3CE; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #611D00; margin-bottom: 10px;">Stuck: Picks Model B</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #611D00; line-height: 1.6;">"Model B has 80% accuracy, Model A only 70%, so Model B wins."</div>
  </div>
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #CCF1D0; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #003F25; margin-bottom: 10px;">Strong: Weighs the Cost of Mistakes</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #003F25; line-height: 1.6;">"Model A catches every real toxic berry (100% recall). Missing one is worse than over-flagging a safe one, so Model A is actually better here."</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Language to borrow:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222;">&bull; Try arguing, hypothetically, that higher accuracy automatically makes a model better, then ask students to push back on that reasoning themselves.</div>
</div>

## The Precision/Recall Trade-Off

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; What to watch for in the threshold widget:</strong> a lower threshold raises recall but lowers precision, students should discover this by actually trying very low and very high thresholds, not just being told. There's no universally correct threshold, the right one depends entirely on which kind of mistake costs more in the context a student picks.
  </div>
</div>

## Assessment: Level 2 Check for Understanding

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; What a strong answer needs:</strong> naming which metric they prioritized (recall or precision) and reasoning about the cost of mistakes in their chosen context, not just picking a number that felt right.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
